course: french
lesson: 18
title: Food and drink
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French food words almost never stand alone: you like le pain (bread in general), but at the shop you ask for du pain (some bread).
explain: When you talk about liking or hating food, use le, la, l' or les: j'aime le chocolat, je n'aime pas les légumes.
explain: When you ask for some of something, use du, de la, de l' or des: du lait, de la confiture, de l'eau, des pommes.
explain: Picture a café in Calais: Charlotte orders des chips, expecting hot chips, and gets a bag of crisps, because in France hot chips are des frites.
explain: Food names often hide a little story: pain perdu, "lost bread", is French toast, made from old bread that is saved instead of thrown away.
fact: J'aime le pain means "I like bread"; after aimer, adorer, détester and préférer you use le, la, l' or les for things in general.
fact: "Some" is du (masculine), de la (feminine), de l' (before a vowel) or des (plural): Je voudrais du pain (I'd like some bread).
fact: des frites are chips (French fries) and des chips are crisps.
fact: un pain au chocolat and une chocolatine are the same pastry; in the south-west of France many people say chocolatine, and people joke about which word is right.
fact: le fromage ("fro-MAZH") is cheese; France has hundreds of cheeses, and in a French meal cheese often comes after the main course and before dessert.
fact: Before a vowel sound, le and la lose their vowel: de l'eau (some water), l'orange.
fact: les escargots are snails cooked in garlic butter, a famous dish many French children eat only on special occasions; Non merci is a polite way to say no.
fact: After an amount French uses de: un kilo de pommes, une bouteille d'eau, un paquet de chips; kilo is masculine.
fact: une fraise is a strawberry, une framboise is a raspberry and une myrtille is a blueberry.
fact: le pain perdu ("lost bread") is French toast: old bread soaked in egg and milk and fried.
fact: Je n'aime pas les légumes means "I don't like vegetables": with like and dislike, les stays even after pas.
fact: In Brittany une crêpe is a thin, usually sweet pancake, and une galette is a buckwheat pancake, usually savoury, with ham, cheese or egg.
fact: Slang here: "la" (Liverpool) means mate; "bare" (London) means lots of; "me mam" (Leeds) means my mum; "wicked" (Boston) means very; "proper lush" (West Country) means really lovely; "my lover" (West Country) means dear; "y'all" (Texas) means you all.
exercise: Olivia says "J'aime pain." What is right? | J'aime le pain. | J'aime du pain. | J'aime un pain. | J'aime de pain.
exercise: At the shop Josh wants some bread. What should he say? | Je voudrais du pain. | Je voudrais le pain. | Je voudrais pain. | Je voudrais un peu pain.
exercise: Which is right for "some jam"? (confiture is feminine) | de la confiture | du confiture | des confiture | de le confiture
exercise: Charlotte wants hot chips. What should she order? | des frites | des chips | des crisps | des pommes
exercise: What are des chips in France? | crisps | hot chips | biscuits | pieces of fish
exercise: The baker in Toulouse says "Une chocolatine ?" What does she mean? | a pain au chocolat | a chocolate bar | a hot chocolate | a chocolate cake
exercise: How do you say le fromage? | "fro-MAZH", soft zh | "FRO-madge", like badge | "fro-MAH-gee" | "FROM-age", like "from age"
exercise: When does cheese often come in a French meal? | after the main course, before dessert | before the soup | only at breakfast | after dessert, with coffee
exercise: Lily writes "de le eau". What is right? | de l'eau | de la eau | du eau | des eau
exercise: What are les escargots? | snails | snakes | sausages | sardines
exercise: Aisha says "Une kilo de pommes." What is right? | Un kilo de pommes. | Une kilo de pommes. | Un kilo des pommes. | Un kilo pommes.
exercise: What is une fraise? | a strawberry | a raspberry | a phrase | a blueberry
exercise: Which fruit is une framboise? | a raspberry | a strawberry | a cherry | a blackberry
exercise: What is le pain perdu? | French toast | bread that went missing | a burnt baguette | a very old loaf
exercise: Mason says "Je n'aime pas des légumes." What is right? | Je n'aime pas les légumes. | Je n'aime pas de légumes. | Je n'aime pas des légume. | Je n'aime les légumes pas.
exercise: In Brittany, which pancake is usually savoury? | une galette | une crêpe au sucre | une crêpe au chocolat | un pain perdu
exercise: How do you say "a bottle of water"? | une bouteille d'eau | une bouteille de l'eau | une bouteille du eau | une bouteille eau
test: Olivia asks, "Why does bread need 'the'?" What's the rule? | After j'aime, use le, la, l' or les for the food | French always says "the" twice | Bread is a special word that needs le | After j'aime, never use any little word
test: Josh asks, "What's 'du' about?" when he gets du pain. What does du mean here? | some | the | two | of the day
test: Charlotte says, "I wanted chips, la!" ("La" means mate.) What went wrong? | In France chips are crisps; hot chips are frites | The café ran out of potatoes | Chips is the French word for fish | She should have said "des chipsies"
test: Ruby asks, "Is chocolatine a different thing?" What's the truth? | It's the same pastry with a local name | It's a hot chocolate in a cup | It's a pain au chocolat with no chocolate | It's a chocolate made only in Belgium
test: Lily says, "Bare letters squeezing together." ("Bare" means lots of.) Why l'eau? | le and la lose their vowel before a vowel sound | eau is a feminine word with no vowel | French drops the e of le before any word with three letters | The apostrophe means "some"
test: Ollie says, "I'm eating snails. Me mam'll go mad." ("Me mam" means my mum.) What is true? | Snails are a special-occasion dish, not food French kids eat every day | All French people eat snails every single day | Snails are the most common school dinner in France | Snails are banned in France
test: At the market a man says "un kilo de pommes". Aisha says, "Still got my apples." What did she get wrong? | kilo is masculine: un kilo | kilo is feminine: une kilo | pommes needs des: de des pommes | Nothing, she was right
test: Leo asks, "Why is strawberries 'fraises'? Sounds like phrases." Which list is all berries? | fraise, framboise, myrtille | fraise, fromage, frites | framboise, pomme de terre, chips | myrtille, escargot, crêpe
test: Grace asks, "Lost bread? Is it missing?" What is the story of le pain perdu? | Old bread is soaked in egg and milk and fried, so it is not wasted | The bread was lost in a forest by a baker, then found again by children | It is bread that you hide for a game | It is bread given to ducks in the park
test: Mason says, "So many little words, wicked hard." ("Wicked" means very.) Which is right? | Je n'aime pas les carottes. | Je n'aime pas des carottes. | Je n'aime pas carottes. | Je n'aime pas de les carottes.
test: Freya's friend orders une galette and is surprised. "Proper lush, my lover," says Freya about her crêpe. ("Proper lush" means really lovely.) What did the friend get? | a savoury buckwheat pancake with ham, cheese or egg | a sweet pancake filled with chocolate, banana and whipped cream | a cake with candles | a biscuit tin
test: ★★★ Which shopping list is all correct? | du lait, de la confiture, de l'eau, des pommes | du lait, du confiture, de l'eau, des pommes | de la lait, de la confiture, de l'eau, des pommes | du lait, de la confiture, de la eau, des pommes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of articles after the verb aimer? | The definite article le, la, l' or les is required before the thing liked | No article whatsoever is employed after aimer | The partitive du or de la is invariably required | The article is employed solely before masculine nouns
exam: Identify the French term denoting what in Britain are called crisps. | des chips | des frites | des croquettes | des galettes
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning le pain perdu. | It denotes stale bread soaked in egg and milk and fried | It denotes bread which has been mislaid | It denotes bread baked solely in Brittany | It denotes bread fed to livestock
exam: It is given that a purchaser requests apples by weight. Determine the correct expression. | Un kilo de pommes | Une kilo de pommes | Un kilo des pommes | Un kilo pommes
